Nil is more strict in lobster, in that a reference type which can
be nil is different from those that are never nil, and you explicitly
convert between them.

a or b and c
(a || b) && c
These are really the only operators that are different.

let a = f() or g()
var a = f();

if (a == null) a = g();
The or operator works slightly differently in that
rather than returning true, it returns whatever value was true (and
true is everything that is not nil, false, 0 or 0.0)

